1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,936
Hi, this is TripRescue, a travel platform that treats your whole trip as one connected system, not five disconnected bookings.

2
00:00:08,936 --> 00:00:19,213
When a flight, hotel, or activity breaks, TripRescue knows exactly what else is affected downstream, and generates real, ranked ways to fix it.

3
00:00:19,213 --> 00:00:21,000
Let me show you.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,216
First, a real account. I'll register with a name, email, and password.

5
00:00:26,216 --> 00:00:35,780
That goes to our FastAPI backend, which checks the email isn't taken, hashes the password with salted PBKDF2, and creates the account.

6
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:42,735
Now I'm on a genuinely empty dashboard, no seeded data, because I haven't earned any yet.

7
00:00:42,735 --> 00:00:47,082
I'll create a trip: Rajasthan Winter Escape, Delhi to Udaipur.

8
00:00:47,082 --> 00:00:53,167
The only fields this form asks for are the name, origin, destination, and dates.

9
00:00:53,167 --> 00:01:03,600
When I save, the backend attaches this trip to my account using the identity from my session token, not anything the browser could fake.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,312
Now let's add the first real itinerary item, a flight.

11
00:01:07,312 --> 00:01:12,055
I'll fill in the airline, confirmation number, airport codes, and schedule.

12
00:01:12,055 --> 00:01:18,523
To be honest with you, this is manual entry today, not a live flight search.

13
00:01:18,523 --> 00:01:24,128
That's the clear next step, not something I'm going to pretend already exists.

14
00:01:24,128 --> 00:01:37,063
What is real: when I save, the backend validates everything, confirms I actually own this trip, and creates the flight and its matching booking record in a single atomic transaction.

15
00:01:37,063 --> 00:01:43,100
And there it is, appearing immediately in the timeline, pulled straight from the database.

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:55,174
To show you a fully built-out itinerary, I'm switching to TripRescue's example account, Aisha's Ladakh Expedition.

17
00:01:55,174 --> 00:02:09,510
Same login, same real backend - this is just a richer, pre-loaded dataset: eight days, flights, a hotel, and activities across Mumbai, Delhi, and Leh.

18
00:02:09,510 --> 00:02:12,950
Here's the Timeline, day by day.

19
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:19,831
Here's the Graph, the actual dependency structure the recovery engine reasons over.

20
00:02:19,831 --> 00:02:29,005
The Map plots every located stop geographically. And Bookings lists every confirmation number in one place.

21
00:02:29,005 --> 00:02:39,900
All four views are reading the exact same trip record from the backend - there's no separate copy anywhere.

22
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:29,307
Now let's break something on purpose. I'll simulate a three-hour delay on the Mumbai to Delhi flight.

23
00:03:29,307 --> 00:03:41,033
To be clear, this disruption and the recovery response are simulated, so you can see exactly how the product behaves during a real interruption.

24
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:47,874
Watch the cascade: the connection in Delhi is now broken, five bookings are downstream-impacted.

25
00:03:47,874 --> 00:03:58,135
Financial exposure jumps to about forty-two thousand eight hundred rupees at risk, all computed live from the actual itinerary, not hardcoded.

26
00:03:58,135 --> 00:04:09,862
TripRescue found three ranked recovery strategies: a premium rebooking scoring 96, a balanced option at 88, and a cheap but 20-hour-late option at 58.

27
00:04:09,862 --> 00:04:15,237
I'll apply the top-ranked one, rebooking onto Air India flight AI-445.

28
00:04:15,237 --> 00:04:21,100
And the itinerary updates immediately: new times, new risk levels, propagated everywhere.

29
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:53,269
A different kind of disruption this time: the Pangong Lake Tour activity gets cancelled.

30
00:04:53,269 --> 00:04:58,566
Again, simulated - but the response is real.

31
00:04:58,566 --> 00:05:11,145
TripRescue found one feasible alternative: rebooking with Ladakh Adventures, scoring 82 out of 100, preserving 6 of 7 commitments.

32
00:05:11,145 --> 00:05:22,400
Worth being honest here too: not every disruption type has a feasible alternative in this demo's dataset.

33
00:05:51,000 --> 00:06:01,052
From here on, nothing is simulated. This is just the real app under normal conditions. I'll refresh the browser.

34
00:06:01,052 --> 00:06:12,161
Both recoveries are still exactly as applied - that's coming fresh from the database on every load, not a client-side cache.

35
00:06:12,161 --> 00:06:18,510
I'll log out and log back in as Aisha - still there.

36
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:25,387
Now the important part: I'll switch to a second, completely separate account, Rohan.

37
00:06:25,387 --> 00:06:31,735
His My Trips page says plainly: you don't have any trips yet.

38
00:06:31,735 --> 00:06:40,200
The backend enforces that by filtering every query against the identity in his own session token.

39
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:32,910
So what's actually running underneath this? React and Vite on the frontend, talking to a FastAPI backend.

40
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:44,462
Every request passes through authentication, an ownership check, and real business logic before touching the database through SQLAlchemy.

41
00:07:44,462 --> 00:07:57,297
Locally, I've verified this entire flow against a real PostgreSQL instance running in Docker, with Alembic managing every schema change.

42
00:07:57,297 --> 00:08:08,207
In production, the backend runs on Render, built to connect to Supabase's managed Postgres the same way.

43
00:08:08,207 --> 00:08:20,400
Every action you've seen follows the same chain: click, API request, authentication, business logic, database transaction, response, UI update.

44
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:30,765
That's TripRescue: real authentication, a real database, a real disruption-and-recovery engine.

45
00:08:30,765 --> 00:08:44,310
Today it fully supports flights end-to-end. Hotels, activities, and live provider search are the clear next step.

46
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:46,700
Thanks for watching.
